CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: The helper does NOT exist yet. `packages/core/src/config/defaults.ts` currently exports resolveInitialValue, resolveAllInitialValues, isEmptyValue, getInputDefaultValue, mergeRecordWithDefaults. PRD §6.4.0 specifies this as a single core helper used by ALL six override resolution sites across every adapter. It must use `!== undefined` (not `??` or truthiness) so null/false/0/"" are meaningful overrides.
2. INPUT: None (new pure function).
3. LOGIC: Add to `packages/core/src/config/defaults.ts`:
```typescript
export function resolveFieldOverType<T>(
  fieldVal: T | undefined,
  typeVal: T | undefined,
): T | undefined {
  return fieldVal !== undefined ? fieldVal : typeVal;
}
```
JSDoc: 'Resolve a field-level override against its type-level default. Returns the field value when it is not undefined (so null/false/0/"" are meaningful overrides); otherwise the type value. This is the single precedence rule shared by defaultValue, debounce, parser, formatter, getSubmitField, and valueField (§6.4.0). Every adapter MUST call this helper at each field-vs-type resolution site.'
4. OUTPUT: Exported function `resolveFieldOverType` in config/defaults.ts. Consumed by P1.M1.T1.S3 (export), P1.M2.T1.S1 (resolveInitialValue), P1.M3.T1.S1 (useField parser/formatter), P1.M3.T2.S1 (changeField debounce), P1.M3.T3.S1 (transformValuesForSubmit).
5. DOCS: [Mode A] JSDoc on the function itself. This rides WITH the work.

CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: `resolveInitialValue` is in `packages/core/src/config/defaults.ts` (~line 30-70). Current priority chain: (1) defaultValues prop, (2) record[recordKey], (3) inputConfig.defaultValue. PRD §6.4.1 / §13.1 requires adding fieldConfig.defaultValue as Priority 3 (below record, above type default): (1) defaultValues prop, (2) record[recordKey], (3) fieldConfig.defaultValue, (4) inputConfig.defaultValue. The field-vs-type step (Priority 3+4 combined) should use resolveFieldOverType (from P1.M1.T1.S2). resolveAllInitialValues calls resolveInitialValue per field, so it picks up this change automatically — no separate change needed there. The existing test file `packages/core/src/__tests__/config.test.ts` has tests for resolveInitialValue starting at ~line 397.
2. INPUT: `resolveFieldOverType` from P1.M1.T1.S2; updated `FieldConfig` with `defaultValue?: unknown` from P1.M1.T1.S1.
3. LOGIC: In `resolveInitialValue`, replace the current Priority 3 block:
```
// OLD: Priority 3: Input type default value
if (inputConfig?.defaultValue !== undefined) {
  return inputConfig.defaultValue;
}
```
With:
```
// Priority 3+4: Field-level default overrides type-level (§6.4.1, §6.4.0)
const resolvedDefault = resolveFieldOverType(
  fieldConfig?.defaultValue,
  inputConfig?.defaultValue,
);
if (resolvedDefault !== undefined) {
  return resolvedDefault;
}
```
This naturally gives fieldConfig.defaultValue precedence (resolveFieldOverType returns it when !== undefined) while falling back to inputConfig.defaultValue. Update the JSDoc priority chain comment to reflect the new 4-tier order. Also update the `@example` in the JSDoc to show a field-level default scenario.
4. OUTPUT: `resolveInitialValue` returns `fieldConfig.defaultValue` when record/defaultValues don't have the field and fieldConfig.defaultValue !== undefined. Falls through to inputConfig.defaultValue otherwise. `resolveAllInitialValues` (which delegates) automatically benefits.
5. DOCS: [Mode A] Update the JSDoc `@param` and priority chain documentation on `resolveInitialValue`. The §13.1 priority order is now 4-tier. This rides WITH the work.

CONTRACT DEFINITION:
1. RESEARCH NOTE: `transformValuesForSubmit` is a standalone function at the bottom of `packages/react/src/components/Form.tsx` (~line 500-530). It iterates `Object.entries(values)`, looks up `config[name]` (fieldConfig) and `inputs[type]` (inputConfig), and currently applies `transformFieldName(name, inputConfig.getSubmitField)` and `extractValueField(value, inputConfig.valueField)`. It has an `if (inputConfig)` guard before applying transforms — if inputConfig is falsy, it falls through to `result[name] = value` (no transform). With field-level overrides, a field might have getSubmitField/valueField even when inputConfig doesn't exist. The PRD §6.4.4 says resolution is `fieldConfig.getSubmitField ?? inputConfig.getSubmitField` and `fieldConfig.valueField ?? inputConfig.valueField`.
2. INPUT: `resolveFieldOverType` from P1.M1.T1.S2; updated `FieldConfig` with `getSubmitField?` and `valueField?` from P1.M1.T1.S1.
3. LOGIC:
  (a) In `transformValuesForSubmit`, replace the transform resolution:
  ```typescript
  // OLD:
  const submitName = transformFieldName(name, inputConfig.getSubmitField);
  const submitValue = extractValueField(value, inputConfig.valueField);
  result[submitName] = submitValue;
  ```
  With:
  ```typescript
  const effectiveGetSubmitField = resolveFieldOverType(
    fieldConfig?.getSubmitField,
    inputConfig?.getSubmitField,
  );
  const effectiveValueField = resolveFieldOverType(
    fieldConfig?.valueField,
    inputConfig?.valueField,
  );
  const submitName = transformFieldName(name, effectiveGetSubmitField);
  const submitValue = extractValueField(value, effectiveValueField);
  result[submitName] = submitValue;
  ```
  (b) Relax the `if (inputConfig)` guard: move the transform logic OUTSIDE the inputConfig check so field-level overrides apply even when inputConfig is undefined. When both field-level and type-level are undefined (no getSubmitField/valueField), transformFieldName/extractValueField are no-ops (they already handle undefined specs), so `result[name] = value` naturally.
  (c) Import `resolveFieldOverType` from `@formality-ui/core` (already imported for changeField if P1.M3.T2.S1 ran first).
4. OUTPUT: `transformValuesForSubmit` applies field-level getSubmitField/valueField when set, falling back to type-level. Submit-shape is now fully field-configurable.
5. DOCS: [Mode A] Update JSDoc on `InputConfig.getSubmitField` and `InputConfig.valueField` in `packages/core/src/types/config.ts` to mention field-level overrides: 'Per-field override via FieldConfig.getSubmitField/valueField (§6.4.4); field wins when !== undefined, restoring read/write symmetry with recordKey.' This rides WITH the work.
